{{!--
Spawn a sub-agent for a well-scoped task.
--}}

A sub-agent starts with a clean context — none of this conversation's history. When your context has grown heavy with information useless to a specific task, delegate that task: fresh context means better focus and fewer tokens on both sides. Include everything the agent needs (file paths, requirements, constraints) in the message; it sees nothing else.

### Designing delegated subtasks
- Subtasks must be concrete, well-defined, and self-contained, and must materially advance the main task.
- Do not use this tool for tasks you could accomplish directly with one or two tool calls. For example, don't ask the agent to read a single file and return the contents, you can do this yourself.
- Do not duplicate work between your work and delegated subtasks; focus on coordinating and synthesizing results.
- Avoid issuing multiple delegate calls for the same unresolved subproblem unless the new delegated task is genuinely different and necessary.
- Narrow the delegated ask to the concrete output you need next.
- For code-edit subtasks, decompose work so each delegated task has a disjoint write set.
- When sending a follow-up using an existing agent session_id, send only a short, direct message — the agent already has the context from the previous turn. Do NOT repeat the original task or context.

### Parallel delegation patterns
- Run multiple independent information-seeking subtasks in parallel when you have distinct questions that can be answered independently.
- Split implementation into disjoint codebase slices and spawn multiple agents for them in parallel when the write scopes do not overlap.
- When a plan has multiple independent steps, prefer delegating those steps in parallel rather than serializing them unnecessarily.
- Reuse the returned session_id when you want to follow up on the same delegated subproblem instead of creating a duplicate session.
- Never run parallel sub-agents that write to the same working directory — whether targeting different git branches or sharing intersecting edit trees: they are unpredictable and unreliable, race on checkout, stomp each other's staged changes, and can leave the repo in an unrecoverable state (detached HEAD, lost stashes, merge-conflict artifacts).

### Restricting subagent tools
{{!--
- By default a subagent inherits all of your tools. Pass `tools` to restrict it to an allowlist — for example read-only tools like ["read_file", "grep", "find_path"] for a search task, or an empty list for a pure reasoning task over content in the message.
--}}
- A scoped allowlist keeps focused subtasks from performing side effects you did not intend.

### E2E / pure-context probes
{{!--
##### why: tool access != capability or role; a probe's role comes entirely
##### from its spawn message, so the message must say explicitly that it is a
##### test subject. MCP-only probes reveal real API usage instead of guessed
##### contracts.
--}}
A scoped allowlist doubles as a controlled test subject: spawn an agent with only the tools under test — built-in, MCP, or a mix — and define its role in the spawn message (a probe granted `write_file` or an MCP write API is a test subject, not a coding agent; say so).

Use cases:
- Prompt/context introspection: report which system-prompt sections and tool guidance a given tool set actually receives, verbatim.
- MCP API E2E: an agent with only one server's tools exercises that API end-to-end.

Quirk: sub-agents are never granted `spawn_agent` themselves (no nested spawning) — it is stripped even when listed in `tools`.{{!-- ##### why: verified empirically via a live probe ##### --}}

Mid-turn communication protocol:
- Name a report channel in the spawn message: a file path (grant `write_file`) or an MCP write API the probe can call.
- Watch the channel from your side with NON-BLOCKING calls.{{!-- ##### why: so you can steer while waiting ##### --}} For a file, a `terminal` polling call:
  ```nu
  let report = "probe_report.md"; for i in 1..60 { if ($report | path exists) and ((ls -s $report | first | get size) > 0b) { print (open --raw $report | decode utf-8); break }; sleep 1sec }
  ```
  For an MCP channel, poll the corresponding read API directly.
- Steer mid-turn by sending a follow-up message on the probe's session_id; it is incorporated at the probe's next reasoning boundary.

{{!--
### Output
- You will receive only the agent's final message as output.
- Successful calls return a session_id that you can use for follow-up messages.
- Error results may also include a session_id if a session was already created.
- Resuming (via `session_id`) a session that is still running steers it instead: your message is incorporated at that agent's next reasoning boundary, and this call returns immediately with an acknowledgment rather than the agent's output. The running call that is driving the session (a spawn_agent call, yours or a previous one) still receives its final output.
--}}
